candidate.summary="Two naturalists contest whether broad well-being can ground moral truth, with Harris strongest on scope and Haidt strongest on psychology, institutions, and plural goods.";
const proMove=moveMap.get("plural-peaks-preserve-objective-error"), conMove=moveMap.get("institutional-reason-corrects-individual-bias");
candidate.quotes.pro={text:proMove.quoteEligibleExactSpans[0],context:"Harris uses multiple flourishing peaks to preserve plural good lives while still allowing objective moral error."};
candidate.quotes.con={text:conMove.quoteEligibleExactSpans[0],context:"Haidt distinguishes unreliable solitary reasoning from the corrective power of properly organized collective inquiry."};
candidate.overall.pro.strengths=[
"Precisely preserved objective error while allowing several genuinely different peaks of human flourishing.",
"Separated evolutionary explanations of psychology from the normative authority of the dispositions evolution produced.",
"Directly exposed the missing exclusivity premise in claims that religion uniquely supplies social goods."
];
candidate.overall.pro.blunders=[
{text:"Asserted that secular agents must be able to build workable institutions without presenting an architecture answering Haidt’s nested-group mechanism.",links:[]},
{text:"Absorbed meaning and narrative into broad well-being largely by definition, leaving their alleged independent value insufficiently tested.",links:[]}
];
candidate.overall.con.strengths=[
"Explained how institutionalized disconfirmation can convert motivated individual reasoning into more reliable collective inquiry.",
"Connected evolved group psychology to a concrete nested-institution model instead of deriving moral norms directly from evolution.",
"Sharply rejected the inference from intuitive moral judgment to the nonexistence of moral truth."
];
candidate.overall.con.blunders=[
{text:"Initially generalized Harris’s ISIS-specific end-zone claim into a broader single-goal account that Harris had not actually asserted.",links:[]},
{text:"Presented equal rights as emergent social truth without adequately explaining why emergence preserves truth rather than contingent acceptance.",links:[]}
];
candidate.logicalExtension.pro.finalArgument={
thesis:"Conscious well-being supplies an objective moral domain when it is treated as a multidimensional, evidence-responsive ordering of possible lives rather than a single pleasure total.",
premises:[
"Any moral system that ignored severe, avoidable conscious suffering would omit a reason whose practical force remains intelligible across cultures, traditions, and evolutionary histories.",
"Plural dimensions of flourishing permit several incomparable or locally optimal lives while still ruling out states that are worse along every morally relevant dimension.",
"Evolutionary and social psychology constrain feasible reforms, but constraints inform the route toward improvement rather than conferring authority on every inherited disposition or institution.",
"Meaning, narrative, rights, and belonging can enter evaluation as durable structures of agency whose loss predictably damages lives, not merely as momentary pleasurable sensations.",
"Institutionalized criticism can compare outcomes, expose motivated reasoning, and revise provisional priorities without pretending that solitary reason mechanically derives policy from abstract first principles."
],
conclusion:"Therefore rational inquiry centered on richly specified well-being can guide moral improvement objectively, although its conclusions remain plural, institution-sensitive, and revisable rather than reducible to one universal metric."
};
candidate.logicalExtension.pro.newArguments=[
{title:"Dominance Without Commensuration",text:"Objective guidance does not require converting every good into a common numerical currency. Some social states can be judged worse because they increase suffering, destroy agency, and weaken stable relationships without improving any competing dimension. This partial ordering answers the measurement objection: moral inquiry can identify dominated outcomes even when two flourishing lives remain incomparable. The account therefore earns practical conclusions where evidence is decisive while withholding false precision where goods trade off. Plural peaks become a feature of objective assessment, not a concession to unrestricted relativism."},
{title:"Reflective Institutional Design",text:"Haidt’s account of institutional reason can strengthen a well-being framework rather than displace it. If individuals predictably rationalize preferred conclusions, moral inquiry should build adversarial review, ideological diversity, transparent outcome measures, and opportunities for affected groups to challenge proposed reforms. These procedures do not generate values from nowhere; they reduce error when investigating which arrangements actually sustain agency, belonging, health, and freedom. A rationalist case is strongest when it specifies such corrective institutions, abandoning the unsupported claim that good secular architecture will simply appear once religion recedes."},
{title:"Substitutable Functions Test",text:"Claims that religion is indispensable should be divided into functions rather than evaluated as an indivisible package. Ritual coordination, mutual aid, intergenerational identity, transcendence, and moral education may each have different secular substitutes and different transition costs. Comparative trials can ask which mechanisms preserve benefits while reducing doctrinal harms, with gradual replacement where abrupt removal would damage cohesion. This approach respects evolved dependence on group practices without granting any existing institution permanent necessity. It converts the debate from wholesale abolition versus inheritance into testable, function-by-function institutional reform."}
];
candidate.logicalExtension.con.finalArgument={
thesis:"Well-being is morally important but insufficient by itself because guidance also depends on constitutive social meanings, historically formed agency, and institutions that make practical reasoning reliable.",
premises:[
"People encounter goods through identities, practices, and relationships that shape what counts as a reason, not as isolated subjects choosing among experiential totals.",
"Meaningful projects can justify sacrifice and diminished pleasure because achievement, fidelity, and participation organize a life across time rather than merely improve remembered affect.",
"Rights function as public commitments among mutually accountable persons, so their authority cannot be captured completely by summing the experiences produced in each immediate case.",
"Evolved group psychology does not dictate morality, yet it constrains which institutions can sustain trust, cooperation, and correction without producing fragmentation or coercive backlash.",
"Because individual reasoning is motivated, legitimate guidance requires plural institutions that expose rival evidence, protect dissent, and test universal proposals against situated human consequences."
],
conclusion:"Thus moral judgment should treat flourishing as one indispensable consideration within a plural, institutionally disciplined framework, not as a sufficient master value that automatically absorbs every other reason."
};
candidate.logicalExtension.con.newArguments=[
{title:"Constitutive Goods",text:"Some goods do not merely cause well-being; they partly define the activities through which a person becomes an accountable agent. Keeping a promise, honoring a relationship, or completing a demanding vocation can matter even when the immediate experiential balance is negative. If these goods are redescribed as well-being only after their independent importance is recognized, the account explains them by relabeling rather than derivation. A pluralist framework can acknowledge their effects on flourishing while retaining the distinct reasons supplied by fidelity, authorship, achievement, and shared practice."},
{title:"Legitimacy Beyond Outcomes",text:"Two policies may produce similar aggregate flourishing yet differ in whether those governed had standing to contest, authorize, or revise them. Legitimate institutions treat persons as participants in common rule, not solely as locations where benefits and harms occur. This adds a procedural moral fact that outcome measurement alone can miss. The point does not make consequences irrelevant: severe suffering can defeat an otherwise legitimate arrangement. It instead explains why rights, representation, and due process remain morally weighty even when benevolent administrators predict better experiential results."},
{title:"Transition-Sensitive Reform",text:"An institution can contain false beliefs and still coordinate trust, obligation, and mutual aid through mechanisms that reformers do not yet understand. Removing it on the strength of destination-level comparisons may create a transition whose costs overwhelm the intended gains. Moral guidance therefore needs path-dependent evaluation: map the functions currently performed, identify replacement institutions, test them at smaller scales, and preserve reversible steps. This argument avoids sanctifying tradition because every function remains criticizable; it requires rational reform to demonstrate a feasible route, not merely a superior imagined endpoint."}
];
